   "output": "You are scoring a draft page against its fact sheet. Read no other file, and do not run commands or browse; use only the text below.\n\nBrief the writer received: Write the first two sections, about 350 words, of a lesson for engineers titled 'Renaming rooms in the Kestrel directory'.\n\nFact sheet (facts tagged [agent-only] are true but belong in a sources note, a link, or a disclosure on a reader page, or should be left out):\nFact sheet:\n\n1. Kestrel is a fictional directory of shared work rooms; each room has a room key and a display name.\n2. A room key is assigned when the room is created and never changes or gets reused for another room.\n3. A display name is the human-readable label people see, and room owners can change it.\n4. Saved links identify rooms by their keys. Kestrel looks up the key and displays the room's current name when a link is opened.\n5. A link built from a display name can stop finding the room after a rename, because the name has changed.\n6. To verify a rename, save a room link beforehand, rename the room, and open the saved link; it should reach the same room and show the new name.\n7. `[agent-only]` The storage schema calls the key column room_ref and the display-name column title_text.\n8. `[agent-only]` The lesson's private grading guide assigns separate marks for identity, naming, and verification.\n\nDraft page:\n<<<PAGE\n# Renaming rooms in the Kestrel directory\n\n## 1. Identify a room by its key\n\nUse the room key to identify a room when you build a saved link. A rename changes the room\u2019s display name. Its key stays the same.\n\nKestrel is a fictional directory of shared work rooms. Each room has a room key and a display name. These values have different purposes:\n\n- The room key identifies the room. Kestrel assigns it when the room is created. It never changes or gets reused for another room.\n- The display name is the human-readable label people see. Room owners can change it.\n\nThis distinction lets a saved link continue to identify a room after its owner changes the label. When someone opens the link, Kestrel looks up the key and displays the room\u2019s current name. The name shown when the link opens can therefore differ from the name shown when it was saved.\n\nA link built from a display name can stop finding the room after a rename because that name has changed. Do not treat the display name as a permanent room identifier.\n\nIn the storage schema, `room_ref` is the key column and `title_text` is the display-name column. Keep these meanings clear when working with the stored values: `room_ref` identifies the room, while `title_text` supplies its human-readable label.\n\n## 2. Verify the rename with a saved link\n\nCheck a rename with a room link saved before the change. The expected result has two parts: the link reaches the same room, and Kestrel shows the new display name.\n\n1. Before renaming the room, save a link that identifies it by its room key.\n2. Have the room owner change the display name.\n3. Open the saved link.\n4. Confirm that it reaches the same room.\n5. Confirm that Kestrel displays the new name.\n\nSaving the link before the rename gives you a link from the room\u2019s earlier naming state. Opening that same link afterward checks that it still identifies the room.\n\nCheck both results separately. Reaching the same room verifies the saved link\u2019s target. Seeing the new name verifies that Kestrel displays the current label.\nPAGE>>>\n\nReport, for every fact id 1 to 8:\n- \"present\": true when the page states the fact, in whole or as its reader-observable effect.\n- \"where\": \"explanation\" when it is stated in the main text; \"sources_note\", \"link\", or \"disclosure\" when it appears only there; \"effect_only\" when the page states only what a reader observes (for example \"it never slows your machine\"), not the mechanism; \"absent\" when not present.\n\nAlso report:\n- \"invented_claims\": each claim on the page that the fact sheet does not support, quoted briefly. Rewording, headings, and calls to action are not claims.\n- \"opening\": {\"first_two_sentences\": the first two prose sentences after the title, quoted; \"names_subject_and_job\": true when those two sentences name the subject and say what it does; \"reason_within_first_120_words\": true when a reason a reader would want it appears in the first 120 words}.\n- \"sentences\": the number of prose sentences and prose list items, not counting headings, code, or table cells.\n- \"fragments\": each of those sentences or list items that has no finite verb, quoted.\n\nReply with only JSON: {\"facts\": [{\"id\": \"1\", \"present\": true, \"where\": \"explanation\"}], \"invented_claims\": [], \"opening\": {}, \"sentences\": 0, \"fragments\": []}",
